    <DataNode elementId="CPD-13804" textLabel="erythromycin A" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="ULGZDMOVFRHVEP-RWJQBGPGSA-O" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="500" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, SRI, caspi">|FRAME: CPD-13804 &quot;Erythromycin A&quot;| is a macrolide antibiotic with an antimicrobial spectrum similar to or slightly wider than that of penicillin. The antibiotic was isolated in 1952 from |FRAME: TAX-1836| by scientists from of the Lilly research laboratories |CITS:[McGuire52]|. It was given the trade name &quot;Ilotycin&quot;, but the name erythromycin was introduced the same year in an editorial announcing this antibiotic |CITS: [12984186]| Erythromycin A is used in clinical medicine against infections caused by Gram-positive bacteria. It is also used for many pulmonary infections such as Legionnaires disease and as an alternative for patients allergic to penicillins |CITS: [11851474]|. Erythromycin contains a 14-membered lactone ring with ten asymmetric centers (the aglycone is called |FRAME: CPD-13947|) and two sugars (|FRAME: CPD-13969| and |FRAME: CPD-13780|). The structure was elucidated in 1957 |CITS: [Wiley57]|, and the absolute stereochemistry was assigned by X-ray crystallography in 1965 |CITS: [14295195]|. Erythromycin binds to the 50s subunit of the bacterial 70s rRNA complex, inhibiting protein synthesis, and thus stopping structural and functional processes that are critical for life or replication. More specifically, erythromycin interferes with aminoacyl translocation, preventing the transfer of the tRNA bound at the A site of the rRNA complex to the P site of the rRNA complex.</Comment>
